<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Olbia</h1><p class="st-meta">550 BCE – 55 BCE (approx.) · capital Olbia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony on the Bug-Dnieper estuary that was the main Greek gateway to the Scythians, described first-hand by Herodotus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> first settled on Berezan island in the 7th century BCE, and around 550 BCE founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia-pontica">Olbia</a> ('the prosperous') on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Hypanis</a> (Southern Bug) estuary. Its people also called it Borysthenes, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. The colony took over much of the fertile surrounding steppe edge.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia-pontica">Olbia</a> traded wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and fine pottery to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> for grain, fish, hides and slaves, and lived alongside Scythian rulers, one of whom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scyles">Scyles</a>, kept a house there. A self-governing polis, it worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-delphinios">Apollo Delphinios</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, and issued early cast bronze money shaped like dolphins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> visited in the 5th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia-pontica">Olbia</a> resisted a siege by Alexander's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zopyrion">Zopyrion</a> c. 331 BCE but declined in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. Around 55 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> sacked it. It was later rebuilt on a smaller scale under Roman protection and abandoned in the 4th century CE. The ruins near Parutyne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> are a protected reserve.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/olbia.jpg" alt="Olbia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Japs 88 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Olbia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 BCE – 55 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Olbia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Ionic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Olbia Pontica, Borysthenes</dd></dl></aside></div>

